-- allium: 3
-- 02-calculation-graph.allium

-- Scope: The content-addressed calculation graph: Veridian's memoization
--   system of record (extreme reuse of results).
-- Includes: model versions and their promotion, calculation requests pinned
--   to a knowledge time, planned steps, immutable calculation nodes keyed by
--   calc_id, memo hits, stampede control, early cutoff, retries, timeouts and
--   ringfenced failure.
-- Excludes:
--   - Bitemporal input versions (01-bitemporal-data.allium).
--   - Identity, rights, audit (03-identity-access.allium).
--   - Worker process supervision and wire protocol (04-julia-worker.allium);
--     here a worker is the external party that fulfils DeterministicComputation.
--   - Instrument maths (05-irs-pricing.allium) and FRTB (06-frtb-girr.allium).
--   - Standing views that re-evaluate when new data arrives (later spec).
-- Dependencies: 01-bitemporal-data.allium.
--
-- Identity rule (the heart of memoization):
--   calc_id = hash(model version identity, as_of, content hashes of the
--                  resolved input versions, result_hash of each prerequisite
--                  node, calculation context)
--   The request's known_at chooses which versions are resolved but is NOT
--   hashed, so identical data reached at different times reuses results.
--   Hashing prerequisite result_hash (not prerequisite calc_id) gives early
--   cutoff: an upstream change that leaves a result byte-identical leaves
--   every downstream calc_id unchanged.
-- Complete nodes are never invalidated: a node is valid forever for exactly
-- its inputs. "Invalidation" is a new request resolving to new calc_ids.

use "./01-bitemporal-data.allium" as data
use "./05-configuration-release.allium" as cfg

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract DeterministicComputation {
    compute: (calc_id: String, model: ModelVersion, as_of: Timestamp, inputs: Set<data/InputVersion>, prerequisite_results: Set<String>) -> String

    @invariant Purity
        -- The result depends only on the arguments: no clock, no environment
        -- variables, no direct database or network reads, no global state.

    @invariant Determinism
        -- Identical arguments produce a byte-identical result and therefore
        -- the same result_hash, on any worker, after any restart.

    @invariant CanonicalInputOrdering
        -- Inputs and prerequisite results are consumed in a canonical order
        -- (sorted by content hash); set iteration order never affects output.

    @invariant CanonicalRounding
        -- result_hash is computed over the canonical form of the result
        -- (D-004, D-015): rounding half-even; monetary amounts to 4 decimal
        -- places of the currency unit; rates and percentages to one hundredth
        -- of a basis point (0.000001 as a decimal rate); discount factors to
        -- 7 significant digits. Year fractions are exact rationals defined by their
        -- day-count convention (ISDA 2006 s4.16) and are never rounded.
        -- Timestamps are UTC with microsecond precision. Two results equal
        -- after canonical rounding have the same result_hash, which drives
        -- early cutoff.

    @invariant VersionedHashScheme
        -- Every calc_id, content_hash and result_hash names the hashing
        -- scheme that produced it (algorithm and canonical encoding version,
        -- D-041). A new scheme arrives only in a reviewed release; existing
        -- identifiers stay valid under their own scheme and are never
        -- rewritten. A node is carried into a new scheme only by recomputing
        -- it and checking that the result is equal after canonical rounding;
        -- the link between the old and new identifiers is recorded.

    @guidance
        -- Random seeds, where needed, are derived from calc_id.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A versioned calculation function (e.g. "irs_pv" 1.2.0) bound to exact code.
-- It arrives only inside a configuration release (05, D-016): four-eyes is
-- the release's code review. Identity is the code, not the release, so a model
-- carried unchanged into the next release keeps its calc_ids.
entity ModelVersion {
    function_name: String
    version: String
    code_hash: String
    first_release: cfg/ConfigurationRelease
    registered_at: Timestamp
}

-- What a user asked for, pinned to the moment it was asked.
entity CalculationRequest {
    requested_by: data/User
    model: ModelVersion
    subjects: Set<data/InputSeries>
    data_class: data/DataClass
    as_of: Timestamp
    known_at: Timestamp
    -- Who or what asked: a screen, an API client, a batch run (06). Free text
    -- reference used for traceability and for batch runs to find their request.
    origin_ref: String?
    requested_at: Timestamp
    status: open | fulfilled | unfulfilled
    completed_at: Timestamp when status = fulfilled | unfulfilled
    failure_reason: String when status = unfulfilled

    transitions status {
        open -> fulfilled
        open -> unfulfilled
        terminal: fulfilled, unfulfilled
    }

    -- True once the planning step's DAG has been turned into evaluation steps.
    plan_expanded: Boolean

    steps: PlannedStep with request = this
    all_done: plan_expanded and steps.all(s => s.status = done)
    any_abandoned: steps.any(s => s.status = abandoned)
}

-- One node of the request's plan. It can only be identified (given a calc_id)
-- once all prerequisites are done, because calc_id includes their result hashes.
entity PlannedStep {
    request: CalculationRequest
    model: ModelVersion
    inputs: Set<data/InputVersion>
    prerequisites: Set<PlannedStep>
    -- planning: the Julia planner computing the DAG (D-020);
    -- evaluation: any node of that DAG (pricing, shocks, sensitivities, ...).
    purpose: planning | evaluation
    status: waiting | running | done | abandoned | cancelled

    calc_id: String when status = running | done | abandoned
    node: CalculationNode when status = running | done | abandoned
    memo_hit: Boolean when status = running | done | abandoned

    transitions status {
        waiting -> running
        waiting -> done
        waiting -> cancelled
        waiting -> abandoned
        running -> done
        running -> abandoned
        terminal: done, abandoned, cancelled
    }

    is_ready: prerequisites.all(p => p.status = done)
    node_available: node.status = complete and node.reproducible and node.payload?.tier in {hot, cold}
    node_failed: node.status = failed or not node.reproducible
    request_failed: request.status = unfulfilled
}

-- The memoized calculation itself. Immutable once complete; shared by every
-- step, in any request, that resolves to the same calc_id.
entity CalculationNode {
    calc_id: String
    model: ModelVersion
    as_of: Timestamp
    inputs: Set<data/InputVersion>
    prerequisite_nodes: Set<CalculationNode>
    data_class: data/DataClass
    created_at: Timestamp
    attempts: Integer
    dispatched_at: Timestamp?
    status: pending | computing | complete | failed

    result_hash: String when status = complete
    completed_at: Timestamp when status = complete
    failure_reason: String when status = failed
    failed_at: Timestamp when status = failed

    transitions status {
        pending -> computing
        computing -> complete
        computing -> pending
        computing -> failed
        terminal: complete, failed
    }

    -- False once a recomputation failed to reproduce result_hash.
    reproducible: Boolean

    payload: ResultPayload with node = this
    steps: PlannedStep with node = this
}

-- Result payload storage (D-005). The node (provenance and result_hash) is
-- kept; only the payload ages out. A purged payload is recomputed on demand
-- and must reproduce the node's result_hash.
entity ResultPayload {
    node: CalculationNode
    tier: hot | cold | purged
    tier_since: Timestamp
    result_ref: String when tier = hot | cold
    -- True while a recomputation of this purged payload is dispatched and not
    -- yet answered (D-062): later steps attach to it, no second one is sent.
    recomputing: Boolean

    transitions tier {
        hot -> cold
        cold -> purged
        purged -> hot
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    max_attempts: Integer = 3
    compute_timeout: Duration = 10.minutes
    -- Development values (D-005); production values configured later.
    hot_retention: Duration = 7.days
    cold_retention: Duration = 30.days
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Model governance (D-016): models are registered when a release activates;
-- there is no runtime submission or approval. Independent validation of each
-- model version, and the gate it puts on production releases, are spec 21
-- (D-050).
rule RegisterReleaseModels {
    when: cfg/ReleaseActivated(release, previous)
    ensures:
        for m in release_models(release):
            if not exists ModelVersion{code_hash: model_code_hash(m)}:
                ModelVersion.created(
                    function_name: model_function_name(m),
                    version: model_version_label(m),
                    code_hash: model_code_hash(m),
                    first_release: release,
                    registered_at: now
                )
}

-- A request is pinned to a knowledge time: now by default, or an earlier
-- instant (batch cut-off D-016, or reproducing what was known in the past).
-- Its subjects must resolve at (as_of, known_at), otherwise it is refused up
-- front (D-008). Production data needs a model in the active production
-- release; golden, synthetic and test may also use the active development
-- release. A whatif request (D-038) uses production models and may name
-- production subjects alongside its author's whatif series.
rule AcceptCalculationRequest {
    -- model: calc_graph.submit
    when: CalculationRequested(requester, model, subjects, data_class, as_of, known_at?, origin_ref?)
    let pinned = known_at ?? now
    requires: pinned <= now
    requires: data/permitted(requester, "calc.request", model)
    requires: subjects.all(s => s.data_class = data_class
        or (data_class = whatif and s.data_class = production))
    requires: subjects.all(s => s.data_class != whatif or s.created_by = requester)
    requires: subjects.all(s => resolvable(s, as_of, pinned))
    requires: model_in_release(model, cfg/active_release(production))
        or (data_class in {golden, synthetic, test} and model_in_release(model, cfg/active_release(development)))
    ensures: CalculationRequest.created(
        requested_by: requester,
        model: model,
        subjects: subjects,
        data_class: data_class,
        as_of: as_of,
        known_at: pinned,
        origin_ref: origin_ref,
        requested_at: now,
        status: open,
        plan_expanded: false
    )
}

-- Planning is itself a memoized Julia calculation (D-018, D-020): its calc_id
-- covers the planner version, the subjects' content and the configuration
-- release, so re-requesting the same thing reuses the plan.
rule StartPlanning {
    when: request: CalculationRequest.created
    ensures: PlannedStep.created(
        request: request,
        model: planner_for(request.model),
        inputs: resolve_all(request.subjects, request.as_of, request.known_at),
        prerequisites: {},
        purpose: planning,
        status: waiting
    )
}

-- One pass (D-020): the plan names every evaluation node with the series it
-- needs and its prerequisites. Elixir resolves the series bitemporally; it
-- never interprets instrument content. Any unresolvable series fails the
-- request before evaluation starts (D-008).
rule ExpandPlan {
    when: step: PlannedStep.status transitions_to done
    requires: step.status = done
    requires: step.purpose = planning
    requires: step.request.status = open
    requires: not step.request.plan_expanded
    let request = step.request
    let entries = plan_entries(step.node)
    ensures:
        if unresolved_series(entries, request.as_of, request.known_at).count > 0:
            PlanningFailed(request: request, reason: "unresolvable input series")
        else:
            request.plan_expanded = true
            for e in entries:
                StepPlanned(
                    request: request,
                    model: entry_model(e),
                    inputs: resolve_all(entry_series(e), request.as_of, request.known_at),
                    prerequisites: entry_prerequisites(e, request)
                )
}

rule AddPlannedStep {
    when: StepPlanned(request, model, inputs, prerequisites)
    requires: request.status = open
    requires: prerequisites.all(p => p.request = request)
    requires: inputs.all(v => v.series.data_class = request.data_class
        or (request.data_class = whatif and v.series.data_class = production))
    ensures: PlannedStep.created(
        request: request,
        model: model,
        inputs: inputs,
        prerequisites: prerequisites,
        purpose: evaluation,
        status: waiting
    )
}

-- Binding a ready step to its node. Exactly one branch applies:
--   complete node  -> memo hit, no computation
--   irreproducible node -> the step is abandoned at once (D-062): the node can
--                          no longer serve results, a recomputation cannot help
--   purged payload -> recomputed once (D-062): the first step starts it and
--                     marks the payload; later steps attach to it
--   in-flight node -> stampede control: attach, no second computation
--   failed node    -> failure is terminal for that calc_id
--   no node        -> miss: exactly one new node is created
-- Steps of a request that has already failed are not bound (they are cancelled).
rule StepBindsToNode {
    -- model: calc_graph.bindNew calc_graph.bindExisting
    when: step: PlannedStep.is_ready
    requires: step.status = waiting
    requires: step.request.status = open
    let id = calc_id_of(step.model, step.request.as_of, step.inputs, prerequisite_result_hashes(step.prerequisites))
    let existing = CalculationNode{calc_id: id}
    ensures:
        if exists existing:
            step.calc_id = id
            step.node = existing
            step.memo_hit = true
            if existing.status = complete and not existing.reproducible:
                step.status = abandoned
            else if existing.status = complete and existing.payload?.tier = purged:
                step.status = running
                if not existing.payload.recomputing:
                    existing.payload.recomputing = true
                    RematerialisationRequested(payload: existing.payload)
            else if existing.status = complete:
                step.status = done
            else if existing.status = failed:
                step.status = abandoned
            else:
                step.status = running
        else:
            let node = CalculationNode.created(
                calc_id: id,
                model: step.model,
                as_of: step.request.as_of,
                inputs: step.inputs,
                prerequisite_nodes: prerequisite_nodes(step.prerequisites),
                -- In a whatif request, a step that touches no whatif input
                -- (directly or through prerequisites) is a production node:
                -- calc_id ignores the class, so it is shared with production.
                data_class: node_class_of(step),
                created_at: now,
                attempts: 0,
                dispatched_at: null,
                status: pending,
                reproducible: true
            )
            step.status = running
            step.calc_id = id
            step.node = node
            step.memo_hit = false
}

-- Dispatch to a worker (on creation and on each retry).
rule DispatchNode {
    -- model: calc_graph.dispatch
    when: node: CalculationNode.status becomes pending
    requires: node.attempts < config.max_attempts
    ensures:
        node.status = computing
        node.attempts = node.attempts + 1
        ComputationDispatched(node: node)
}

-- The compute timeout runs from the moment a worker takes the job, not from
-- dispatch: time spent in the bounded queue (07) never counts against it.
rule ComputationStartsOnWorker {
    when: ComputationAssigned(node)
    requires: node.status = computing
    ensures: node.dispatched_at = now
}

rule ComputationSucceeds {
    -- model: calc_graph.succeed
    when: ComputationSucceeded(worker, node, result_ref, result_hash)
    requires: node.status = computing
    ensures:
        node.status = complete
        node.result_hash = result_hash
        node.completed_at = now
        node.dispatched_at = null
        ResultPayload.created(node: node, tier: hot, tier_since: now, result_ref: result_ref, recomputing: false)
}

rule PayloadMovesToColdStorage {
    -- model: calc_graph.cool
    when: payload: ResultPayload.tier_since + config.hot_retention <= now
    requires: payload.tier = hot
    ensures:
        payload.tier = cold
        payload.tier_since = now
}

rule PayloadPurged {
    -- model: calc_graph.purge
    when: payload: ResultPayload.tier_since + config.cold_retention <= now
    requires: payload.tier = cold
    ensures:
        payload.tier = purged
        payload.tier_since = now
        payload.result_ref = null
}

-- A purged result is needed again: recompute it and prove determinism by
-- matching the recorded result_hash. Stampede control applies: concurrent
-- requests for the same purged payload trigger one recomputation: the first
-- step binding to the payload sets `recomputing` (StepBindsToNode), so this
-- rule fires once per recomputation (D-062).
rule RematerialisationDispatched {
    -- model: calc_graph.bindExisting calc_graph.oneRematInFlight
    when: RematerialisationRequested(payload)
    requires: payload.tier = purged
    ensures: RecomputationDispatched(payload: payload)
}

rule RematerialisationSucceeds {
    -- model: calc_graph.rematAnswer
    when: RematerialisationCompleted(worker, payload, result_ref, result_hash)
    requires: payload.tier = purged
    requires: payload.node.status = complete
    ensures:
        payload.recomputing = false
        if result_hash = payload.node.result_hash:
            payload.tier = hot
            payload.tier_since = now
            payload.result_ref = result_ref
        else:
            ReproducibilityBreachDetected(node: payload.node, observed_hash: result_hash)
}

-- A breach is a model-risk incident: the node can no longer serve results.
rule MarkNodeIrreproducible {
    -- model: calc_graph.rematAnswer
    when: ReproducibilityBreachDetected(node, observed_hash)
    requires: node.reproducible
    ensures: node.reproducible = false
}

-- Infrastructure failure (crash, OOM kill, lost worker): retry while allowed.
rule ComputationCrashes {
    -- model: calc_graph.crash
    when: ComputationCrashed(worker, node, reason)
    requires: node.status = computing
    ensures:
        if node.attempts < config.max_attempts:
            node.status = pending
            node.dispatched_at = null
        else:
            node.status = failed
            node.failure_reason = reason
            node.failed_at = now
            node.dispatched_at = null
}

-- Deterministic rejection (invalid inputs, model error): retrying is futile.
rule ComputationRejects {
    -- model: calc_graph.reject
    when: ComputationRejected(worker, node, reason)
    requires: node.status = computing
    ensures:
        node.status = failed
        node.failure_reason = reason
        node.failed_at = now
        node.dispatched_at = null
}

rule ComputationTimesOut {
    -- model: calc_graph.crash
    when: node: CalculationNode.dispatched_at + config.compute_timeout <= now
    requires: node.status = computing
    ensures:
        ComputationCrashed(worker: null, node: node, reason: "timeout")
}

rule StepCompletesWithNode {
    -- model: calc_graph.stepDone
    when: step: PlannedStep.node_available
    requires: step.status = running
    requires: not step.node_failed
    ensures: step.status = done
}

-- Ringfence: a failure reaches only the steps bound to that node.
rule StepFailsWithNode {
    -- model: calc_graph.stepAbandon
    when: step: PlannedStep.node_failed
    requires: step.status = running
    ensures: step.status = abandoned
}

-- Waiting steps that can no longer run are cancelled. Running siblings keep
-- going: their nodes are shared and may serve other requests.
rule StepCancelledWithRequest {
    -- model: calc_graph.stepCancel
    when: step: PlannedStep.request_failed
    requires: step.status = waiting
    ensures: step.status = cancelled
}

rule RequestCompletes {
    -- model: calc_graph.requestFulfil
    when: request: CalculationRequest.all_done
    requires: request.status = open
    requires: not request.any_abandoned
    ensures:
        request.status = fulfilled
        request.completed_at = now
}

rule RequestFails {
    -- model: calc_graph.requestFail
    when: request: CalculationRequest.any_abandoned
    requires: request.status = open
    ensures:
        request.status = unfulfilled
        request.completed_at = now
        request.failure_reason = "calculation step abandoned"
}

-- Cancellation by the requester, or by a batch operator for a batch run
-- (D-038). Waiting steps are cancelled; running nodes finish, since they are
-- shared and may serve other requests.
rule CancelRequest {
    -- model: calc_graph.cancel
    when: RequestCancellationRequested(user, request)
    requires: request.status = open
    requires: user = request.requested_by
        or data/permitted(user, "calc.cancel", request.origin_ref)
    ensures:
        request.status = unfulfilled
        request.completed_at = now
        request.failure_reason = "cancelled"
}

-- Up-front failure (D-008): an input with no version effective at as_of, as
-- known at known_at, has no content hash, so no calc_id can exist. The planner
-- reports it before any step or node is created.
rule PlanningFails {
    when: PlanningFailed(request, reason)
    requires: request.status = open
    requires: not request.plan_expanded
    ensures:
        request.status = unfulfilled
        request.completed_at = now
        request.failure_reason = reason
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant OneNodePerCalcId {
    -- model: calc_graph.oneNodePerCalcId
    for a in CalculationNodes:
        for b in CalculationNodes:
            a != b implies a.calc_id != b.calc_id
}

-- Data classes never mix: no test or synthetic input reaches a production
-- result, and vice versa. The one-way whatif exception (D-038): a whatif node
-- may read production inputs and nodes; nothing else reads whatif.
invariant NodeInputsShareDataClass {
    for n in CalculationNodes:
        n.inputs.all(v => v.series.data_class = n.data_class
            or (n.data_class = whatif and v.series.data_class = production))
}

invariant WhatifNeverLeaks {
    for n in CalculationNodes:
        n.data_class != whatif
            implies n.prerequisite_nodes.all(p => p.data_class != whatif)
}

-- A production node's model came through a reviewed production release.
invariant ProductionUsesReviewedModels {
    for n in CalculationNodes:
        n.data_class = production implies released_for_production(n.model)
}

invariant AttemptsBounded {
    -- model: calc_graph.attemptsBounded
    for n in CalculationNodes:
        n.attempts <= config.max_attempts
}

-- No recomputation is in flight for a node already known irreproducible (D-062).
invariant NoRecomputationOfIrreproducibleNode {
    -- model: calc_graph.noRematOnBroken
    for p in ResultPayloads:
        p.recomputing implies p.node.reproducible
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Requester {
    identified_by: data/User
}

-- The supervised Julia worker pool (04-julia-worker.allium).
actor ComputeWorker {
    identified_by: data/User
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface CalculationRequests {
    facing requester: Requester
    context request: CalculationRequest where requested_by = requester

    exposes:
        request.status
        request.as_of
        request.known_at
        for s in request.steps:
            s.status

    provides:
        CalculationRequested(requester, model, subjects, data_class, as_of, known_at?, origin_ref?)
        RequestCancellationRequested(requester, request) when request.status = open
}

surface ModelCatalogue {
    facing requester: Requester
    context model: ModelVersion

    exposes:
        model.function_name
        model.version
        model.code_hash
        model.first_release.tag
}

surface WorkerProtocol {
    facing worker: ComputeWorker
    context node: CalculationNode where status = computing

    exposes:
        node.calc_id
        node.model
        node.as_of
        node.inputs
        node.prerequisite_nodes

    provides:
        ComputationSucceeded(worker, node, result_ref, result_hash)
        ComputationCrashed(worker, node, reason)
        ComputationRejected(worker, node, reason)

    contracts:
        demands DeterministicComputation

    timeout:
        ComputationTimesOut
}

surface WorkerRecomputation {
    facing worker: ComputeWorker
    context payload: ResultPayload where tier = purged

    exposes:
        payload.node.calc_id
        payload.node.model
        payload.node.as_of
        payload.node.inputs

    provides:
        RematerialisationCompleted(worker, payload, result_ref, result_hash)

    contracts:
        demands DeterministicComputation
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deferred Specifications
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Black boxes owned by Julia (D-018), read by Elixir only as opaque data:
--   planner_for(model)          planner model version for a function, from the
--                               tagged configuration release
--   plan_entries(node)          the DAG in a completed planning node's payload
--   entry_model / entry_series / entry_prerequisites(e, request)
--                               fields of one DAG entry; prerequisites map the
--                               plan's local ids to this request's steps
-- Black boxes owned by Elixir:
--   resolvable(series, as_of, known_at), resolve_all(series_set, as_of,
--   known_at), unresolved_series(entries, as_of, known_at): via
--   data/resolve_version.
--   calc_id_of(...), prerequisite_result_hashes(...), prerequisite_nodes(...):
--   calc_id_of is a canonical, collision-resistant hash of hashes (D-018).
--   node_class_of(step): the request's class, except in a whatif request
--   where a step with no whatif input and no whatif prerequisite is
--   production (D-038).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Resolved: canonical rounding (D-004, D-015), up-front failure (D-008),
-- development retention 7 days hot then 30 days cold, 37 days in total (D-005).
open question "Is the stored payload itself canonically rounded, or kept at full precision with only result_hash computed on the rounded form?"open question "Provenance record: exactly which fields must an auditor see for a reported figure (requester, known_at, model code_hash, every input version, worker build)?"
open question "A failed node is terminal for its calc_id. Should an operator be able to reset an infrastructure-failed node, given calc_id identity?"
open question "Worker capacity and priority: how are dispatched nodes ordered when the bounded queue is full (e.g. regulatory EOD runs first)?"
-- Resolved (D-062), found by the calculation-graph model (models/calc_graph.qnt, D-061): a purged payload is recomputed once however many steps wait for it (the payload carries a recomputing flag), and a step binding to an irreproducible node is abandoned at binding.
open question "A recomputation that is lost (worker crash, timeout) never answers, so with the single recomputing flag (D-062) the waiting steps would wait for ever. A lost recomputation must clear the flag and be retried like a computation (max_attempts), and when attempts run out the steps waiting for the payload must fail. Not yet specified or modelled: confirm and add the rule and the model action."
